5. WHAT THESE ROWS MAY AND MAY NOT SUPPORT
------------------------------------------
MAY support (conditional, descriptive, about the run record itself):
  - the per-row measurements as recorded: prompt/cached/completion tokens,
    ttft_s, latency_s, request_sha256, content/content_sha256, arm, rep, turn;
  - that a run with run_id longrun-2026-11-09T084217Z-bounded-context-fetch-r1
    loaded a TX-AGG document whose bytes hashed 2b416d82... at that moment;
  - the answers the model actually emitted and their grading against the
    expected values recorded inside each row.

MAY NOT support:
  - any reproducibility claim: the named fixture cannot be produced, so the
    prompt prefix (and every token count that depends on it) cannot be rebuilt
    or re-measured;
  - any claim about the TX-AGG fixture currently on disk: it is a different
    document with different ground truths; its numbers are NOT these rows'
    numbers and must never be substituted for them;
  - any cross-arm comparability: all rows carry claim="none" and
    comparison_eligible=false, and the fixture identity is unestablished;
  - any "ABSENT means 0" reading: the fixture is missing, not empty.

6. WHY NOT "RECOVERABLE"
------------------------
recoverable would require an on-disk document with the same content under a
different name or schema. There is none: no byte match in 56,431 files, no
canonicalization match, and the sole same-named candidate disagrees on
ground-truth answers. A mapping row-digest -> that path would be false.

7. WHY NOT "DELETED" (committed)
--------------------------------
The bytes existed and were destroyed, but no commit and no ref ever held them;
classifying it as a commit-recorded deletion would invent provenance. The
accurate state is: provenance broken by an unversioned in-place rewrite at
2026-11-09T08:57:28Z, therefore unverifiable.

8. TEST CONSEQUENCE (do not "fix")
----------------------------------
experiments/ptr-engine/longrun/tests/test_lost_fixture.py::
test_no_new_row_pins_a_missing_fixture is correctly red for these 278 rows.
Its baseline freezes only the older lost digest
72c3832d95f9c7966b4358eda10fa2882775a416ee8a663d57b1c871ed19c44d (259 rows);
2b416d82... is a separate, genuinely-new miss. Honouring a mapping is NOT
justified here (there is no content to map to). Freezing these 278 rows in the
baseline would record the loss without repairing it — that decision belongs to
the unit that owns the test, not to this note.
